  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Addressing Displacement Crises Focusing On Advancing the Secretary General&#x2019;s Action Agenda on Internal Displacement</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The world is experiencing a growing frequency, protractedness and scale of humanitarian crises, increasingly shaped by global challenges, such as the impact of climate change, heightened protection risks, socioeconomic inequalities, and the spread of epidemics. Today, IOM supports more emergencies than at any other time in its history, assisting over 31.1 million crisis-affected people (in 2022). As humanitarian needs grow substantially year on year, so is the number of internally displaced persons (IDPs). By the end of 2022, more than 70 million people were internally displaced worldwide, with women, children and marginalized groups often experiencing the greatest impacts (Internal Displacement Monitoring Centre, 2023).With the overall objective to contribute to addressing displacement crises while advancing its commitment to the Secretary General&#x2019;s Action Agenda on Internal Displacement, through this project, IOM aims to achieve the following outcomes: 1) Solutions initiatives are coordinated and informed by evidence; and 2) IOM&#x2019;s response to displacement crisis is more timely, effective, predictable and principled.</narrative>
  </description>
